<h2>The setting</h2>
<p>The parties&rsquo; relationship arose from an agreement of association for the design, construction and commissioning of a major tunnel road project. When disputes surfaced, the petitioner moved the High Court under Section 11 seeking constitution of the tribunal. At the hearing, the respondent&rsquo;s counsel informed the court that its arbitrator had already been nominated in terms of the agreement&rsquo;s arbitration clause — a clause providing for the familiar two-nominees-plus-presiding structure.</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>From the order (para 5):</strong> with the nomination made, &ldquo;nothing further survives to adjudicate in the matter&rdquo; — the court&rsquo;s only direction was that the nominees appoint the presiding arbitrator within thirty days.</p>
</div>

<h2>The principle at work</h2>
<p>Section 11 is a default-correcting power, not a first port of call. The scheme of the Act — and of most institutional and ad hoc clauses — is that the parties&rsquo; own machinery constitutes the tribunal: each side names its arbitrator, and the two nominees select the presiding member. The court steps in only where that machinery jams. The corollary, illustrated here, is that the machinery can unjam even after a petition is filed: a nomination made before the hearing leaves the court with nothing to appoint, and the petition serves, in substance, as the pressure that completed the clause&rsquo;s operation.</p>

<h2>Timelines that matter in tribunal constitution</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>Step</th><td>Statutory/ordered time</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Respondent&rsquo;s nomination after request</th><td>30 days under the Act&rsquo;s default scheme</td></tr>
  <tr><th>Presiding arbitrator by the two nominees</th><td>30 days from their appointment under the default scheme; 30 days from the order in this case</td></tr>
  <tr><th>If either step fails</th><td>Section 11 petition for court appointment</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Practical notes</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>For claimants.</strong> Issue a precise Section 21 notice and nomination demand, diarise the thirty-day mark, and file under Section 11 promptly on default — the filing itself often produces the nomination, as this case shows.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For respondents.</strong> A nomination made even at the eleventh hour can keep the choice of arbitrator in your hands rather than the court&rsquo;s. Delaying past the hearing risks losing that control entirely.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For both.</strong> Once the two nominees are in place, do not let the presiding appointment drift: the thirty-day direction here is judicially enforceable, and a further default simply invites another trip to court with costs consequences.</div>
</div>
<p>The order is brief, but its message for infrastructure and construction disputes is practical: the fastest route to a working tribunal is usually the clause itself, operated punctually — with Section 11 held in reserve as the enforcement mechanism that keeps everyone honest.</p>
